<body>
<div class="container">
<a href="index.html" class="back-link">&larr; Back to Study Tools</a>
<div class="exam-header">
<h1>Leifer Test Bank &mdash; Maternity Exam 3</h1>
<p class="subtitle">192 items (<b>38 SATA</b>) &middot; MC Exam 3, Tue 10/27 &middot; Ch 15, 21, 22, 24, 25, 26, 34 &middot; this is the peds block</p>
</div>
<div class="exam-info"><b>You said you heard this was the hardest maternity exam, and the reason is real:</b> this is where the course stops being about the mother and becomes about the child. Six of these seven chapters are pediatrics. The assessment skills you built for pregnancy and birth do not transfer cleanly, and that is what catches people.<br><br>Every item here is from the <b>Leifer <i>Introduction to Maternity and Pediatric Nursing</i>, 10th edition</b> test bank &mdash; your edition &mdash; for <b>Ch 15, 21, 22, 24, 25, 26 and 34</b>, which is the exact Exam 3 list off your professor's alignment document. <b>192 questions.</b><br><br><b>Ch 34 almost did not make it in here.</b> None of your exam folders had a source for it &mdash; no textbook excerpt, no cookbook, and the study guide stops at Ch 27. The 20 items you see under Ch 34 came out of the full all-chapters bank instead. Without that, a whole tested chapter would have been a blind spot.<br><br><b>One attempt, no retake.</b> Maternity exams do not get a second run the way Med Surg does. That is exactly why the reps happen here, where a wrong answer costs you nothing but a rationale.<br><br><b>SATA is scored the way she scores it.</b> There are <b>38 select-all items</b> here. Some of the right answers and no wrong ones earns half credit. One wrong pick zeroes the item.<br><br><b>On the rationales.</b> The Leifer bank writes short &mdash; usually one sentence explaining the key, without arguing the wrong options down. So under most distractors you get the bank's actual explanation of the item plus a prompt to say out loud why the key beats what you picked. Nothing here is invented; where the bank stayed silent, the page says so.</div>
<div class="svg-card">
<h3>Must-Know Chart &mdash; What Each Chapter Is Actually Testing</h3>
<svg viewBox="0 0 820 318" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" font-family="DM Sans, sans-serif"><rect width="820" height="318" fill="#0c0f14"/><text x="410" y="26" fill="#7dd3fc" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Exam 3 &mdash; seven chapters, one line each</text><line x1="20" y1="34" x2="800" y2="34" stroke="#334155"/><rect x="18" y="46" width="784" height="34" rx="6" fill="#13181f" stroke="#1e293b"/><text x="30" y="67" fill="#7dd3fc" font-size="12" font-weight="700">Ch 15</text><text x="92" y="60" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">Growth, development, nutrition</text><text x="92" y="74" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="11.5">Development runs head to toe and center outward &middot; play is how you assess a child &middot; milestones are ranges, not deadlines</text><rect x="18" y="84" width="784" height="34" rx="6" fill="#13181f" stroke="#1e293b"/><text x="30" y="105" fill="#7dd3fc" font-size="12" font-weight="700">Ch 21</text><text x="92" y="98" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">The hospitalized child</text><text x="92" y="112" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="11.5">Separation anxiety peaks in toddlers: protest, then despair, then detachment &middot; keep the parents there &middot; regression under stress is normal</text><rect x="18" y="122" width="784" height="34" rx="6" fill="#13181f" stroke="#1e293b"/><text x="30" y="143" fill="#7dd3fc" font-size="12" font-weight="700">Ch 22</text><text x="92" y="136" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">Health care adaptations</text><text x="92" y="150" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="11.5">Weigh the child before you dose anything &middot; restraints are the last resort and get checked often &middot; prepare little ones right before, not days ahead</text><rect x="18" y="160" width="784" height="34" rx="6" fill="#13181f" stroke="#1e293b"/><text x="30" y="181" fill="#7dd3fc" font-size="12" font-weight="700">Ch 24</text><text x="92" y="174" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">Musculoskeletal (peds)</text><text x="92" y="188" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="11.5">Neurovascular check below every cast &middot; nothing goes down inside a cast, ever &middot; a scoliosis brace is worn most of the day, not just at night</text><rect x="18" y="198" width="784" height="34" rx="6" fill="#13181f" stroke="#1e293b"/><text x="30" y="219" fill="#7dd3fc" font-size="12" font-weight="700">Ch 25</text><text x="92" y="212" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">Respiratory (peds)</text><text x="92" y="226" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="11.5">Epiglottitis: nothing in the mouth, keep her calm and upright &middot; croup barks and likes humidity &middot; RSV spreads by contact, not droplets alone</text><rect x="18" y="236" width="784" height="34" rx="6" fill="#13181f" stroke="#1e293b"/><text x="30" y="257" fill="#7dd3fc" font-size="12" font-weight="700">Ch 26</text><text x="92" y="250" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">Cardiovascular (peds)</text><text x="92" y="264" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="11.5">Count the apical pulse for a full minute before digoxin &middot; vomiting is the first sign of digoxin toxicity &middot; Kawasaki risks coronary aneurysm</text><rect x="18" y="274" width="784" height="34" rx="6" fill="#13181f" stroke="#1e293b"/><text x="30" y="295" fill="#7dd3fc" font-size="12" font-weight="700">Ch 34</text><text x="92" y="288" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700">Complementary therapies</text><text x="92" y="302" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="11.5">Always ask what else she is taking &middot; herbal does not mean harmless &middot; several common herbs are unsafe in pregnancy &mdash; document everything the family uses</text></svg>
</div>
<div class="ref-box">
<h4 style="color:#fbbf24">Peds emergencies &mdash; first move</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Epiglottitis:</b> drooling, tripod position, muffled voice, high fever, no cough. <b>Do not put anything in that mouth</b> &mdash; no tongue blade, no throat swab. Keep her calm and upright and get help. Crying can close the airway.</li>
<li><b>Stridor at rest:</b> that is upper airway obstruction, not croup settling down. Oxygen and call.</li>
<li><b>Dehydration in an infant:</b> sunken fontanel, no tears, dry mouth, fewer wet diapers, poor skin turgor. Infants dry out far faster than adults &mdash; weight is the most accurate measure.</li>
<li><b>Digoxin toxicity:</b> vomiting is usually the FIRST sign in a child, before any rhythm change. Hold the dose and report.</li>
<li><b>Cast neurovascular change:</b> pain, pallor, coolness, tingling, or a toe that will not move. Do not wait to see if it improves.</li>
<li class="dim">On peds questions, the answer that keeps the child calm is usually also the answer that keeps the airway open.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="ref-box">
<h4 style="color:#a78bfa">Match the stage to what the child is actually afraid of</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Infant (under 1):</b> stranger anxiety, and disruption of routine. Keep the primary caregiver present.</li>
<li><b>Toddler (1 to 3):</b> separation from parents &mdash; the biggest fear at this age. Protest, despair, then detachment.</li>
<li><b>Preschooler (3 to 6):</b> body mutilation and magical thinking. She may believe she got sick because she was bad. Use bandages generously.</li>
<li><b>School-age (6 to 12):</b> loss of control and falling behind peers. Give her choices and explanations.</li>
<li><b>Adolescent:</b> body image and separation from friends, not from parents. Privacy matters more than comfort.</li>
<li class="dim">If a question gives you an age, the age IS the clue. Read it before you read the options.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="ref-box">
<h4 style="color:#38bdf8">Words that look the same and are not</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Croup vs epiglottitis:</b> croup is viral, gradual, barking cough, low fever, responds to humidity. Epiglottitis is bacterial, sudden, drooling, no cough, high fever &mdash; and it is an emergency.</li>
<li><b>Cyanotic vs acyanotic defects:</b> cyanotic means unoxygenated blood reaches the body (tetralogy of Fallot). Acyanotic means the shunt runs left to right (VSD, ASD, PDA) and she is pink but overloading the lungs.</li>
<li><b>Bronchiolitis vs bronchitis:</b> bronchiolitis is RSV in infants, wheezing and retractions. Bronchitis is older children, cough-dominant.</li>
<li><b>Kawasaki vs rheumatic fever:</b> Kawasaki has no known cause and threatens the coronary arteries. Rheumatic fever follows a strep infection and threatens the heart valves.</li>
<li><b>Cephalocaudal vs proximodistal:</b> head to toe vs center of the body outward. Both are normal directions of development; the exam asks which one a described milestone demonstrates.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="ref-box">
<h4 style="color:#22c55e">Numbers this unit actually tests</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Anterior fontanel:</b> closes at <b>12 to 18 months</b>. Posterior closes by <b>2 to 3 months</b>.</li>
<li><b>Digoxin:</b> count the apical pulse for a <b>full minute</b>. Hold and report if under <b>90 to 110</b> in an infant, under <b>70</b> in an older child.</li>
<li><b>Birth weight:</b> doubles by about <b>6 months</b>, triples by <b>1 year</b>.</li>
<li><b>First teeth:</b> around <b>6 months</b>; about <b>6 new teeth a year</b> after that.</li>
<li><b>Solid foods:</b> start around <b>6 months</b>, one new food at a time, <b>4 to 7 days</b> apart to catch a reaction.</li>
<li><b>No whole milk, no honey:</b> before <b>1 year</b>. Honey carries botulism risk in infants.</li>
<li><b>Scoliosis brace:</b> worn most of the day &mdash; typically <b>18 to 23 hours</b>, over a snug t-shirt.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="ref-box">
<h4 style="color:#64748b">Answers that are almost never right</h4>
<ul>
<li class="dim">&ldquo;Examine the throat&rdquo; or &ldquo;obtain a throat culture&rdquo; in suspected epiglottitis.</li>
<li class="dim">&ldquo;Explain the procedure several days in advance&rdquo; to a toddler or preschooler. Prepare little children right before.</li>
<li class="dim">&ldquo;Tell the parents to leave so the child settles.&rdquo; Separation is the problem, not the fix.</li>
<li class="dim">&ldquo;Insert a padded object to scratch&rdquo; under a cast.</li>
<li class="dim">&ldquo;Give the digoxin&rdquo; when the child just vomited.</li>
<li class="dim">&ldquo;Reassure her the herbal supplement is natural and safe.&rdquo;</li>
<li class="dim">&ldquo;Compare the child to a sibling&rdquo; or to a growth-chart number in isolation.</li>
<li class="dim">&ldquo;Restrain the child&rdquo; as a first choice for anything.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="last-attempt" id="lastAttempt">
Last attempt: <span class="score-val" id="lastScore">&mdash;</span> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <span id="lastDate">&mdash;</span>
<div class="hist-wrap" id="histPanelTop"></div>
</div>
<div class="progress-wrap"><div class="progress-bar" id="progressBar" style="width:0%"></div></div>
