{t:'A 15-year-old adolescent with type 1 diabetes mellitus',c:true,r:'Correct. Adolescents usually do better in rooms with one or more roommates than in single rooms. The adolescent would do best with a roommate who is closest to his or her age and also lives with a chronic illness.'},
{t:'To assign the adolescent to a private room',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Adolescents usually do better in rooms with one or more roommates than in single rooms. The adolescent would do best with a roommate who is closest to his or her age and also lives with a chronic illness.</i> The keyed answer is <b>A 15-year-old adolescent with type 1 diabetes mellitus</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child\'s Experience of Hospitalization</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 21 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning'},

{ch:'Ch 21',topic:'Hospitalized child',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The parents of a hospitalized 9-month-old infant ask if their preschool child may visit his younger sibling. What understanding would assist the nurse most in formulating a response?',
opts:[
{t:'Preschool children can be disruptive in the hospital environment.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Siblings are affected by a child\'s hospitalization. Their ability to cope is influenced by their age, experience, and intactness of the family.</i> The keyed answer is <b>The preschooler needs to visit his infant sister to reassure himself that she is all right.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Seeing his younger sibling would probably frighten the preschooler and thus should be avoided.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Siblings are affected by a child\'s hospitalization. Their ability to cope is influenced by their age, experience, and intactness of the family.</i> The keyed answer is <b>The preschooler needs to visit his infant sister to reassure himself that she is all right.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'The sibling could view the infant from the doorway but not enter the room to prevent the spread of microorganisms.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Siblings are affected by a child\'s hospitalization. Their ability to cope is influenced by their age, experience, and intactness of the family.</i> The keyed answer is <b>The preschooler needs to visit his infant sister to reassure himself that she is all right.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'The preschooler needs to visit his infant sister to reassure himself that she is all right.',c:true,r:'Correct. Siblings are affected by a child\'s hospitalization. Their ability to cope is influenced by their age, experience, and intactness of the family.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child\'s Experience of Hospitalization</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 21 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 21',topic:'Hospitalized child',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'A hospitalized toddler was drinking from a cup at home but now refuses to drink from anything except his favorite bottle. What is the most likely reason for this behavior?',
opts:[
{t:'He is dealing with the anxiety of hospitalization by regressing.',c:true,r:'Correct. Hospitalization is frustrating for toddlers. They show their displeasure when illness restricts satisfaction of their desires. It is not unusual for a toddler who was drinking from a cup to refuse it in the hospital.'},
{t:'He is demonstrating attention-seeking behaviors because of an overabundance of attention in the hospital.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Hospitalization is frustrating for toddlers. They show their displeasure when illness restricts satisfaction of their desires. It is not unusual for a toddler who was drinking from a cup to refuse it in the hospital.</i> The keyed answer is <b>He is dealing with the anxiety of hospitalization by regressing.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'He is attempting to refocus the attention of the adults around him to avoid further painful procedures.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Hospitalization is frustrating for toddlers. They show their displeasure when illness restricts satisfaction of their desires. It is not unusual for a toddler who was drinking from a cup to refuse it in the hospital.</i> The keyed answer is <b>He is dealing with the anxiety of hospitalization by regressing.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'He is exhibiting normal behavior for his age, as children often stop new behaviors after they believe they have mastered them.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Hospitalization is frustrating for toddlers. They show their displeasure when illness restricts satisfaction of their desires. It is not unusual for a toddler who was drinking from a cup to refuse it in the hospital.</i> The keyed answer is <b>He is dealing with the anxiety of hospitalization by regressing.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child\'s Experience of Hospitalization</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 21 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

