{ch:'Ch 21',topic:'Hospitalized child',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse is preparing to obtain a throat culture on a toddler patient. What interventions are appropriate for the nurse to implement? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Model desired behavior.',c:true,r:'Correct. Whenever possible the parent should be involved in the preparation for and initiation of a treatment or procedure, and the child should be prepared according to his or her developmental level. For a toddler, model the behavior desired (i.e., opening the mouth), tell the child it is okay to yell if the treatment or procedure is uncomfortable, and use distractions. Explaining the procedure in detail and encouraging questions are appropriate interventions for an older child.'},
{t:'Instruct patient not to yell.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Whenever possible the parent should be involved in the preparation for and initiation of a treatment or procedure, and the child should be prepared according to his or her developmental level. For a toddler, model the behavior desired (i.e., opening the mouth), tell the child it is okay to yell if the treatment or procedure is uncomfortable, and use distractions. Explaining the procedure in detail and encouraging questions are appropriate interventions for an older child.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Model desired behavior. / Use distractions.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Use distractions.',c:true,r:'Correct. Whenever possible the parent should be involved in the preparation for and initiation of a treatment or procedure, and the child should be prepared according to his or her developmental level. For a toddler, model the behavior desired (i.e., opening the mouth), tell the child it is okay to yell if the treatment or procedure is uncomfortable, and use distractions. Explaining the procedure in detail and encouraging questions are appropriate interventions for an older child.'},
{t:'Explain the procedure in detail.',c:false,r:'Explaining the procedure in detail and encouraging questions are appropriate interventions for an older child.'},
{t:'Encourage the child to ask questions.',c:false,r:'Explaining the procedure in detail and encouraging questions are appropriate interventions for an older child.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child\'s Experience of Hospitalization</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 21 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Intervention'},

{ch:'Ch 21',topic:'Hospitalized child',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'Parents are preparing their child for admission to the pediatric unit for minor surgery. What should they expect to see when visiting the pediatric unit? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Nurses wearing all white',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Availability of a playroom / Colored bedding</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Formal atmosphere',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Availability of a playroom / Colored bedding</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Availability of a playroom',c:true,r:'Correct. The children\'s hospital unit differs in many respects from adult divisions. The pediatric unit or hospital is designed to meet the needs of children and their parents. A cheerful, casual atmosphere helps to bridge the gap between home and hospital and is in keeping with the child\'s emotional, developmental, and physical needs. Nurses wear colorful uniforms, and colored bedspreads and wagons or strollers for transportation provide a more homelike atmosphere. The physical structure of the unit includes furniture of the proper height for the child, soundproof ceilings, and color schemes with eye appeal. Most pediatric departments include a playroom.'},
{t:'Dim lighting',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Availability of a playroom / Colored bedding</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Colored bedding',c:true,r:'Correct. The children\'s hospital unit differs in many respects from adult divisions. The pediatric unit or hospital is designed to meet the needs of children and their parents. A cheerful, casual atmosphere helps to bridge the gap between home and hospital and is in keeping with the child\'s emotional, developmental, and physical needs. Nurses wear colorful uniforms, and colored bedspreads and wagons or strollers for transportation provide a more homelike atmosphere. The physical structure of the unit includes furniture of the proper height for the child, soundproof ceilings, and color schemes with eye appeal. Most pediatric departments include a playroom.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child\'s Experience of Hospitalization</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 21 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning'},

{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'What is the best pulse location for the nurse to use when assessing the pulse rate on a 12-month-old infant?',
opts:[
{t:'Brachial',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Apical pulses are advised for children under age 5 years.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Apical</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Apical',c:true,r:'Correct. Apical pulses are advised for children under age 5 years.'},
{t:'Radial',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Apical pulses are advised for children under age 5 years.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Apical</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Femoral',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Apical pulses are advised for children under age 5 years.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Apical</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse preparing to administer medication to a 2-month-old infant discovers there is no ID bracelet on the child. What should be the next action by the nurse?',
opts:[
{t:'Give the medication after confirming the child\'s name from the foot of the crib.',c:false,r:'After confirmation of the child\'s identity with the charge nurse and making a new bracelet, the medication can be safely given.'},
{t:'Ask the charge nurse to give the medicine.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>After confirmation of the child\'s identity with the charge nurse and making a new bracelet, the medication can be safely given. All patients should be identified before treatment.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Confirm the identity with the charge nurse, make a new bracelet, and give the medicine.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Confirm the identity with the charge nurse, make a new bracelet, and give the medicine.',c:true,r:'Correct. After confirmation of the child\'s identity with the charge nurse and making a new bracelet, the medication can be safely given. All patients should be identified before treatment.'},
{t:'Delay the medication until the admissions office can supply a new ID bracelet.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>After confirmation of the child\'s identity with the charge nurse and making a new bracelet, the medication can be safely given. All patients should be identified before treatment.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Confirm the identity with the charge nurse, make a new bracelet, and give the medicine.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse instructed an adolescent female about collecting a clean-catch urine specimen. What statement made by the adolescent led the nurse to determine she understood the instructions?',
opts:[
{t:'"I should wash my perineum with soap and water, then begin to urinate."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>To obtain a clean-catch specimen, the perineum is cleansed with an antiseptic wipe from front to back.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"I clean the perineum from front to back with an antiseptic wipe before I urinate."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'"I clean the perineum from front to back with an antiseptic wipe before I urinate."',c:true,r:'Correct. To obtain a clean-catch specimen, the perineum is cleansed with an antiseptic wipe from front to back.'},
{t:'"I\'ll collect the first stream of urine in a sterile container."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>To obtain a clean-catch specimen, the perineum is cleansed with an antiseptic wipe from front to back.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"I clean the perineum from front to back with an antiseptic wipe before I urinate."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'"I will discard the first void and collect a freshly voided specimen 30 minutes later."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>To obtain a clean-catch specimen, the perineum is cleansed with an antiseptic wipe from front to back.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"I clean the perineum from front to back with an antiseptic wipe before I urinate."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
