{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The prescription for a 4-month-old is penicillin G 150,000 units intramuscularly bid. The drug is supplied as a unit dose of 600,000 units in a 5-mL vial. How many milliliters (mL) should the nurse provide?',
opts:[
{t:'1.25',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>This dose would have to be given in divided doses as only 0.5 to 1 mL should be injected in one site on an infant.</i> The keyed answer is <b>1.4</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'1.4',c:true,r:'Correct. This dose would have to be given in divided doses as only 0.5 to 1 mL should be injected in one site on an infant.'},
{t:'1.6',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>This dose would have to be given in divided doses as only 0.5 to 1 mL should be injected in one site on an infant.</i> The keyed answer is <b>1.4</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'1.8',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>This dose would have to be given in divided doses as only 0.5 to 1 mL should be injected in one site on an infant.</i> The keyed answer is <b>1.4</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning<br><br><b style="color:#fca5a5">&#9888; THE BANK&rsquo;S KEY IS WRONG ON THIS ONE.</b> The math does not match the key. 150,000 &divide; 600,000 &times; 5 mL = <b>1.25 mL</b>, which is option a, not the 1.4 mL the bank keys. The printed rationale never addresses volume at all &mdash; it argues that a dose this size gets split because only 0.5&ndash;1 mL goes into one infant IM site. Do the arithmetic and trust it.'},

