{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'An infant\'s dry diaper weighs 2.5 g. The wet diaper weighs 47 g. How would the nurse record the infant\'s urine output?',
opts:[
{t:'47 mL',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Urine output is determined by calculating the difference in weight between the wet diaper and a dry diaper. One gram is equivalent to one milliliter of output. 47 – 2.5 = 44.5 g = 44.5 mL of urine.</i> The keyed answer is <b>44.5 mL</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'44.5 mL',c:true,r:'Correct. Urine output is determined by calculating the difference in weight between the wet diaper and a dry diaper. One gram is equivalent to one milliliter of output. 47 – 2.5 = 44.5 g = 44.5 mL of urine.'},
{t:'43.5 mL',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Urine output is determined by calculating the difference in weight between the wet diaper and a dry diaper. One gram is equivalent to one milliliter of output. 47 – 2.5 = 44.5 g = 44.5 mL of urine.</i> The keyed answer is <b>44.5 mL</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'40.5 mL',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Urine output is determined by calculating the difference in weight between the wet diaper and a dry diaper. One gram is equivalent to one milliliter of output. 47 – 2.5 = 44.5 g = 44.5 mL of urine.</i> The keyed answer is <b>44.5 mL</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
