{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse instructs the mother of a 2-year-old child who is taking iron supplements for anemia that some foods reduce the absorption of iron. What would be the best example provided by the nurse?',
opts:[
{t:'Red meat',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Egg yolks and starches reduce the absorption of iron in the digestive tract and should be limited for persons taking an iron supplement.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Egg yolks</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Green, leafy vegetables',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Egg yolks and starches reduce the absorption of iron in the digestive tract and should be limited for persons taking an iron supplement.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Egg yolks</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Acidic fruit juices',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Egg yolks and starches reduce the absorption of iron in the digestive tract and should be limited for persons taking an iron supplement.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Egg yolks</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Egg yolks',c:true,r:'Correct. Egg yolks and starches reduce the absorption of iron in the digestive tract and should be limited for persons taking an iron supplement.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The pediatric nurse completes an assessment on all patients assigned during evening shift at the hospital. Which patient assessment requires immediate intervention?',
opts:[
{t:'Toddler with an axillary temperature of 99°F',c:false,r:'An axillary temperature of 99°F, infant pulse of 100 bpm, and adolescent respiratory rate of 28 are expected assessments.'},
{t:'School-age child with widening pulse pressure',c:true,r:'Correct. A widening pulse pressure can indicate increased ICP; therefore, it is the priority. An axillary temperature of 99°F, infant pulse of 100 bpm, and adolescent respiratory rate of 28 are expected assessments.'},
{t:'Infant pulse rate of 100 beats/minute',c:false,r:'An axillary temperature of 99°F, infant pulse of 100 bpm, and adolescent respiratory rate of 28 are expected assessments.'},
{t:'Adolescent with a respiratory rate of 28 breaths/minute',c:false,r:'An axillary temperature of 99°F, infant pulse of 100 bpm, and adolescent respiratory rate of 28 are expected assessments.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'A 15-year-old patient returns to the pediatric unit following a lumbar puncture. What initial position will the nurse maintain for this patient?',
opts:[
{t:'Left side-lying',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The adolescent may avoid post–lumbar puncture headache by lying flat for some time.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Supine</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Supine',c:true,r:'Correct. The adolescent may avoid post–lumbar puncture headache by lying flat for some time.'},
{t:'Prone',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The adolescent may avoid post–lumbar puncture headache by lying flat for some time.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Supine</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Semi-Fowler\'s',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The adolescent may avoid post–lumbar puncture headache by lying flat for some time.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Supine</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'Informed consent for a minor guarantees that the parent or legal guardian understands what aspect(s) of a procedure? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Purpose of the procedure',c:true,r:'Correct. The informed consent establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands the purpose and risks of the procedure. It also establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands what they have been told; the document should be signed and witnessed.'},
{t:'Risks associated with the procedure',c:true,r:'Correct. The informed consent establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands the purpose and risks of the procedure. It also establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands what they have been told; the document should be signed and witnessed.'},
{t:'That no suit can be brought for damages',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The informed consent establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands the purpose and risks of the procedure. It also establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands what they have been told; the document should be signed and witnessed.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Purpose of the procedure / Risks associated with the procedure / That the document must be signed and witnessed / That information was given</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'That the document must be signed and witnessed',c:true,r:'Correct. The informed consent establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands the purpose and risks of the procedure. It also establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands what they have been told; the document should be signed and witnessed.'},
{t:'That information was given',c:true,r:'Correct. The informed consent establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands the purpose and risks of the procedure. It also establishes that the patient, parent, or legal guardian understands what they have been told; the document should be signed and witnessed.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'Which specific drug(s) should be checked with a second licensed nurse prior to administration? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Insulin',c:true,r:'Correct. Insulin, hypoglycemics, narcotics, digoxin, inotropic drugs, anticoagulants, potassium, and calcium salts all must be checked by a licensed nurse prior to administration.'},
{t:'Digoxin',c:true,r:'Correct. Insulin, hypoglycemics, narcotics, digoxin, inotropic drugs, anticoagulants, potassium, and calcium salts all must be checked by a licensed nurse prior to administration.'},
{t:'Vasodilators',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Insulin, hypoglycemics, narcotics, digoxin, inotropic drugs, anticoagulants, potassium, and calcium salts all must be checked by a licensed nurse prior to administration.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Insulin / Digoxin / Calcium salts / Anticoagulants</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Calcium salts',c:true,r:'Correct. Insulin, hypoglycemics, narcotics, digoxin, inotropic drugs, anticoagulants, potassium, and calcium salts all must be checked by a licensed nurse prior to administration.'},
{t:'Anticoagulants',c:true,r:'Correct. Insulin, hypoglycemics, narcotics, digoxin, inotropic drugs, anticoagulants, potassium, and calcium salts all must be checked by a licensed nurse prior to administration.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'A 3-year-old patient is admitted to the pediatric unit with a fever of 103°F. Which actions will the nurse implement? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Assess rectal temperature every 4 hours.',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Administer acetaminophen as ordered. / Assess skin turgor. / Assess level of consciousness.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Administer acetaminophen as ordered.',c:true,r:'Correct. When evaluating the degree of illness in a febrile child, the nurse should assess and record response of the child to cuddling, alertness, hydration, sociability, and quality of cry. A quiet, lethargic child who does not respond readily to the environment may be acutely ill. Because dehydration is a common problem in infants and children, skin turgor should be assessed. Antipyretics also provide comfort and may aid in enabling the child to consume fluids, lessening the risk of dehydration. Rectal temperatures are not recommended for pediatric patients.'},
{t:'Assess skin turgor.',c:true,r:'Correct. When evaluating the degree of illness in a febrile child, the nurse should assess and record response of the child to cuddling, alertness, hydration, sociability, and quality of cry. A quiet, lethargic child who does not respond readily to the environment may be acutely ill. Because dehydration is a common problem in infants and children, skin turgor should be assessed. Antipyretics also provide comfort and may aid in enabling the child to consume fluids, lessening the risk of dehydration. Rectal temperatures are not recommended for pediatric patients.'},
{t:'Restrict fluids.',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Administer acetaminophen as ordered. / Assess skin turgor. / Assess level of consciousness.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Assess level of consciousness.',c:true,r:'Correct. When evaluating the degree of illness in a febrile child, the nurse should assess and record response of the child to cuddling, alertness, hydration, sociability, and quality of cry. A quiet, lethargic child who does not respond readily to the environment may be acutely ill. Because dehydration is a common problem in infants and children, skin turgor should be assessed. Antipyretics also provide comfort and may aid in enabling the child to consume fluids, lessening the risk of dehydration. Rectal temperatures are not recommended for pediatric patients.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 22',topic:'Health care adaptations',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'What should the nurse assess to determine the method of transportation for a pediatric patient? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Age',c:true,r:'Correct. The means by which the child is transported within the unit and to other parts of the hospital depends on age, level of consciousness, and how far the child must travel.'},
{t:'Race',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The means by which the child is transported within the unit and to other parts of the hospital depends on age, level of consciousness, and how far the child must travel.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Age / Distance to travel / Level of consciousness</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Vital signs',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The means by which the child is transported within the unit and to other parts of the hospital depends on age, level of consciousness, and how far the child must travel.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Age / Distance to travel / Level of consciousness</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Distance to travel',c:true,r:'Correct. The means by which the child is transported within the unit and to other parts of the hospital depends on age, level of consciousness, and how far the child must travel.'},
{t:'Level of consciousness',c:true,r:'Correct. The means by which the child is transported within the unit and to other parts of the hospital depends on age, level of consciousness, and how far the child must travel.'}],
teach:'<b>Health Care Adaptations for the Child and Family</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 22 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'What would the nurse include in planning teaching to parents of a child with Legg-Calvé-Perthes disease about the long-term effects of this disease?',
opts:[
{t:'There are no long-term effects.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Marked distortion of the head of the femur may lead to an imperfect joint or to degenerative arthritis of the hip later in life.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Degenerative arthritis may develop later in life.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'The disease is self-limited and requires no long-term treatment.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Marked distortion of the head of the femur may lead to an imperfect joint or to degenerative arthritis of the hip later in life.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Degenerative arthritis may develop later in life.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Degenerative arthritis may develop later in life.',c:true,r:'Correct. Marked distortion of the head of the femur may lead to an imperfect joint or to degenerative arthritis of the hip later in life.'},
{t:'There is risk of osteogenic sarcoma in adulthood.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Marked distortion of the head of the femur may lead to an imperfect joint or to degenerative arthritis of the hip later in life.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Degenerative arthritis may develop later in life.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'What intervention will the nurse caring for a child in Buck skin traction implement?',
opts:[
{t:'Position in high Fowler\'s position.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Buck traction is a type of skin traction that relies on the child\'s weight as counterbalance. The child must be kept with head elevated no more than 20 degrees and pulled up in bed, and the feet should not touch the bed surface or the foot of the bed.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Assist the child to be pulled up in bed.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Assist the child to be pulled up in bed.',c:true,r:'Correct. Buck traction is a type of skin traction that relies on the child\'s weight as counterbalance. The child must be kept with head elevated no more than 20 degrees and pulled up in bed, and the feet should not touch the bed surface or the foot of the bed.'},
{t:'Keep child\'s heel on the bed surface.',c:false,r:'The child must be kept with head elevated no more than 20 degrees and pulled up in bed, and the feet should not touch the bed surface or the foot of the bed.'},
{t:'Maintain child\'s feet against the foot of the bed.',c:false,r:'The child must be kept with head elevated no more than 20 degrees and pulled up in bed, and the feet should not touch the bed surface or the foot of the bed.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'What will the nurse include when caring for a child in Buck\'s extension?',
opts:[
{t:'Positioning the child with hips flexed 90 degrees at all times',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The skin exposed to frequent friction may break down.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Checking for skin irritation from traction equipment</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Keeping the weights in contact with the floor',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The skin exposed to frequent friction may break down.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Checking for skin irritation from traction equipment</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Checking for skin irritation from traction equipment',c:true,r:'Correct. The skin exposed to frequent friction may break down.'},
{t:'Releasing the weights on a schedule',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The skin exposed to frequent friction may break down.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Checking for skin irritation from traction equipment</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse is reviewing the characteristics of Ewing\'s sarcoma. Which statement if made by the nurse indicates correct understanding of this disease?',
opts:[
{t:'"Amputation is the accepted treatment."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Ewing\'s sarcoma is sensitive to radiation therapy and chemotherapy. Amputation of the affected extremity is not recommended. This cancer occurs in school-age children and does metastasize.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"The disease is sensitive to radiation and chemotherapy."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'"The disease is sensitive to radiation and chemotherapy."',c:true,r:'Correct. Ewing\'s sarcoma is sensitive to radiation therapy and chemotherapy. Amputation of the affected extremity is not recommended. This cancer occurs in school-age children and does metastasize.'},
{t:'"Metastasis is rare."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Ewing\'s sarcoma is sensitive to radiation therapy and chemotherapy. Amputation of the affected extremity is not recommended. This cancer occurs in school-age children and does metastasize.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"The disease is sensitive to radiation and chemotherapy."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'"The disease is more prevalent among toddlers and preschoolers."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Ewing\'s sarcoma is sensitive to radiation therapy and chemotherapy. Amputation of the affected extremity is not recommended. This cancer occurs in school-age children and does metastasize.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"The disease is sensitive to radiation and chemotherapy."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
