{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse assessing a child with juvenile rheumatoid arthritis notes the child\'s right knee and ankle are swollen, warm, and tender. The child has a temperature of 38.8°C (102°F) and abdominal pain. What type of juvenile rheumatoid arthritis do these findings suggest?',
opts:[
{t:'Psoriatic',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The systemic form of juvenile rheumatoid arthritis is associated with an elevated temperature, erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), and C-reactive protein; abdominal pain; and a macular rash.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Systemic</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Enthesitis',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The systemic form of juvenile rheumatoid arthritis is associated with an elevated temperature, erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), and C-reactive protein; abdominal pain; and a macular rash.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Systemic</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Systemic',c:true,r:'Correct. The systemic form of juvenile rheumatoid arthritis is associated with an elevated temperature, erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), and C-reactive protein; abdominal pain; and a macular rash.'},
{t:'Acute febrile',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The systemic form of juvenile rheumatoid arthritis is associated with an elevated temperature, erythrocyte sedimentation rate (ESR), and C-reactive protein; abdominal pain; and a macular rash.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Systemic</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse is providing instructions about how to treat a sprained ankle. What statement by the mother does the nurse recognize as indicative of a need for additional teaching?',
opts:[
{t:'"Apply warm compresses to the ankle for the first 24 hours."',c:true,r:'Correct. Heat is not a treatment for soft tissue injuries. The principles of managing soft tissue injuries are rest, ice, compression, and elevation.'},
{t:'"Put an ice pack on the ankle, alternating 30 minutes on with 30 minutes off."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Heat is not a treatment for soft tissue injuries. The principles of managing soft tissue injuries are rest, ice, compression, and elevation.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"Apply warm compresses to the ankle for the first 24 hours."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'"Wrap the ankle in an Ace bandage for support."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Heat is not a treatment for soft tissue injuries. The principles of managing soft tissue injuries are rest, ice, compression, and elevation.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"Apply warm compresses to the ankle for the first 24 hours."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'"Keep the leg elevated when sitting."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Heat is not a treatment for soft tissue injuries. The principles of managing soft tissue injuries are rest, ice, compression, and elevation.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"Apply warm compresses to the ankle for the first 24 hours."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Evaluation'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'How does Russell traction provide adequate skin traction?',
opts:[
{t:'Subluxates the tibia.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Russell traction is skin traction, similar to Buck, with a sling positioned under the knee, which prevents subluxation of the tibia. Although the traction interferes with full ROM, the patient can change position without disrupting the continuous pull in two directions.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Supplies continuous pull in two directions.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Does not interfere with range of motion.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Russell traction is skin traction, similar to Buck, with a sling positioned under the knee, which prevents subluxation of the tibia. Although the traction interferes with full ROM, the patient can change position without disrupting the continuous pull in two directions.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Supplies continuous pull in two directions.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Prevents the knee from flexing.',c:false,r:'Russell traction is skin traction, similar to Buck, with a sling positioned under the knee, which prevents subluxation of the tibia.'},
{t:'Supplies continuous pull in two directions.',c:true,r:'Correct. Russell traction is skin traction, similar to Buck, with a sling positioned under the knee, which prevents subluxation of the tibia. Although the traction interferes with full ROM, the patient can change position without disrupting the continuous pull in two directions.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse is checking for capillary refill on a child in Bryant\'s traction. How long does it take for the toe to regain color if adequate perfusion is assessed?',
