{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'Which observation is most likely to cause the nurse to consider the possibility of child abuse when a mother says that her young child fell down the basement stairs?',
opts:[
{t:'Red, green, and yellow bruises on his body.',c:true,r:'Correct. As bruises heal, they change color in stages. Different colors of bruises indicate that injuries have not all occurred at the same time. The nurse must consider whether the bruises match the caretaker\'s explanation of what happened.'},
{t:'Bruises are dispersed on his head, arms, and legs.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>As bruises heal, they change color in stages. Different colors of bruises indicate that injuries have not all occurred at the same time. The nurse must consider whether the bruises match the caretaker\'s explanation of what happened.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Red, green, and yellow bruises on his body.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'A broken arm last year, and the child being described as accident-prone.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>As bruises heal, they change color in stages. Different colors of bruises indicate that injuries have not all occurred at the same time. The nurse must consider whether the bruises match the caretaker\'s explanation of what happened.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Red, green, and yellow bruises on his body.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'The mother is very anxious for her son to get medical attention.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>As bruises heal, they change color in stages. Different colors of bruises indicate that injuries have not all occurred at the same time. The nurse must consider whether the bruises match the caretaker\'s explanation of what happened.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Red, green, and yellow bruises on his body.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'A 6-year-old sustained a fractured femur and was put in Russell traction 2 days ago. She screams in pain when she raises herself onto the bedpan. Which nursing diagnosis takes highest priority for this child?',
opts:[
{t:'Pain resulting from tissue trauma',c:true,r:'Correct. Although all of these nursing diagnoses are relevant to the child in traction, pain resulting from muscle spasm and tissue trauma is the highest priority.'},
{t:'High risk for impaired skin integrity resulting from immobility',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Although all of these nursing diagnoses are relevant to the child in traction, pain resulting from muscle spasm and tissue trauma is the highest priority.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Pain resulting from tissue trauma</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Altered growth and development related to separation from family',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Although all of these nursing diagnoses are relevant to the child in traction, pain resulting from muscle spasm and tissue trauma is the highest priority.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Pain resulting from tissue trauma</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Altered urinary elimination related to immobility and traction',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Although all of these nursing diagnoses are relevant to the child in traction, pain resulting from muscle spasm and tissue trauma is the highest priority.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Pain resulting from tissue trauma</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Nursing Diagnosis'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'What would the nurse consider an abnormal finding on a musculoskeletal assessment of a 4-year-old child?',
opts:[
{t:'Has inward-turned knees while standing.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Toe walking after 3 years of age may indicate a muscle problem.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Walks on the toes.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Walks on the toes.',c:true,r:'Correct. Toe walking after 3 years of age may indicate a muscle problem.'},
{t:'Appears to have flat feet.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Toe walking after 3 years of age may indicate a muscle problem.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Walks on the toes.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Swings his arms when walking.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Toe walking after 3 years of age may indicate a muscle problem.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Walks on the toes.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'Why does a child\'s fracture heal more rapidly than the adult\'s?',
opts:[
{t:'A child\'s bones are less porous than adult bone.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Callus forms more rapidly in the child than the adult.</i> The keyed answer is <b>A child\'s bones have faster callus formation.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'A child\'s bones are covered by a thicker periosteum.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Callus forms more rapidly in the child than the adult.</i> The keyed answer is <b>A child\'s bones have faster callus formation.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'A child\'s bones are not affected by bone overgrowth.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Callus forms more rapidly in the child than the adult.</i> The keyed answer is <b>A child\'s bones have faster callus formation.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'A child\'s bones have faster callus formation.',c:true,r:'Correct. Callus forms more rapidly in the child than the adult.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Musculoskeletal Condition</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 24 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 24',topic:'Musculoskeletal (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
