opts:[
{t:'Discoloration of tooth enamel',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Inhalant powders can cause candidiasis (yeast) infection of the mouth.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Candidiasis</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Halitosis',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Inhalant powders can cause candidiasis (yeast) infection of the mouth.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Candidiasis</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Irritation of oral membranes',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Inhalant powders can cause candidiasis (yeast) infection of the mouth.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Candidiasis</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Candidiasis',c:true,r:'Correct. Inhalant powders can cause candidiasis (yeast) infection of the mouth.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse is caring for a 3-year-old child who suffered a smoke inhalation injury. How long is this patient at the highest risk for pulmonary edema after exposure?',
opts:[
{t:'2 hours',c:false,r:'Pulmonary edema appears in a child with smoke inhalation injury 6 to 72 hours after exposure.'},
{t:'4 hours',c:false,r:'Pulmonary edema appears in a child with smoke inhalation injury 6 to 72 hours after exposure.'},
{t:'18 hours',c:false,r:'Pulmonary edema appears in a child with smoke inhalation injury 6 to 72 hours after exposure.'},
{t:'72 hours',c:true,r:'Correct. Pulmonary edema appears in a child with smoke inhalation injury 6 to 72 hours after exposure.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'Which is the most appropriate nursing action when planning care for a child with cystic fibrosis?',
opts:[
{t:'Provide chest physiotherapy before meals every day.',c:false,r:'Chest physiotherapy should be done between meals.'},
{t:'Assess weight monthly.',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Ensure high-protein, high-calorie diet.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Administer pancreas with protein food at mealtime.',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Ensure high-protein, high-calorie diet.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Ensure high-protein, high-calorie diet.',c:true,r:'Correct. The maintenance of adequate nutrition is essential. The diet is high in protein and calories. Chest physiotherapy should be done between meals. Pancreatic enzyme powder should be given with applesauce or other nonstarch, nonfat, nonprotein food. Children with cystic fibrosis should be weighed daily.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The first child of a couple is being treated for bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD). They ask how to prevent this from happening with the child they are currently expecting. What will the nurse explain as the best way to prevent BPD?',
opts:[
{t:'Maternal intake of folic acid',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Prevention of preterm birth</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Exercise',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Prevention of preterm birth</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Prevention of preterm birth',c:true,r:'Correct. Bronchopulmonary dysplasia (BPD) is a fibrosis, or thickening, of the alveolar walls and the bronchiolar epithelium. It occurs in premature infants (less than 32 weeks) who have abnormal or arrested lung development and receive ventilation and oxygen for more than 28 days to survive. Respiratory distress in the newborn is the major reason why oxygen and ventilators are used for prolonged periods. The main cause of respiratory distress in the newborn is prematurity. Therefore, the prevention of preterm birth is the best way to prevent BPD.'},
{t:'Provision of oxygen therapy to the newborn',c:false,r:'Respiratory distress in the newborn is the major reason why oxygen and ventilators are used for prolonged periods.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse describes the "allergic salute" as a cluster of what signs related to chronic allergy? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Mouth breathing',c:true,r:'Correct. The allergic salute does not include a productive cough.'},
{t:'Transverse nasal crease',c:true,r:'Correct. The allergic salute does not include a productive cough.'},
{t:'Dark circles under the eyes',c:true,r:'Correct. The allergic salute does not include a productive cough.'},
{t:'Productive cough',c:false,r:'The allergic salute does not include a productive cough.'},
{t:'Reddened conjunctiva',c:true,r:'Correct. The allergic salute does not include a productive cough.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse would suggest the parents of an asthmatic child to encourage participation in which sport(s)? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Swimming',c:true,r:'Correct. Sports that require bursts of energy rather than long-term output of energy are suitable pursuits for asthmatics. Swimming, gymnastics, and baseball fit this criterion.'},
{t:'Gymnastics',c:true,r:'Correct. Sports that require bursts of energy rather than long-term output of energy are suitable pursuits for asthmatics. Swimming, gymnastics, and baseball fit this criterion.'},
{t:'Baseball',c:true,r:'Correct. Sports that require bursts of energy rather than long-term output of energy are suitable pursuits for asthmatics. Swimming, gymnastics, and baseball fit this criterion.'},
{t:'Cross-country skiing',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Sports that require bursts of energy rather than long-term output of energy are suitable pursuits for asthmatics. Swimming, gymnastics, and baseball fit this criterion.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Swimming / Gymnastics / Baseball</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Distance running',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Sports that require bursts of energy rather than long-term output of energy are suitable pursuits for asthmatics. Swimming, gymnastics, and baseball fit this criterion.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Swimming / Gymnastics / Baseball</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse reports which assessments that suggest a meconium ileus in a newborn? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Abdominal distention',c:true,r:'Correct. Distended abdomen, vomiting, and absence of stool are the signs indicating meconium ileus in the newborn.'},
{t:'Vomiting',c:true,r:'Correct. Distended abdomen, vomiting, and absence of stool are the signs indicating meconium ileus in the newborn.'},
{t:'Hiccoughing',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Distended abdomen, vomiting, and absence of stool are the signs indicating meconium ileus in the newborn.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Abdominal distention / Vomiting / Absence of stool</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Jaundice',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Distended abdomen, vomiting, and absence of stool are the signs indicating meconium ileus in the newborn.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Abdominal distention / Vomiting / Absence of stool</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Absence of stool',c:true,r:'Correct. Distended abdomen, vomiting, and absence of stool are the signs indicating meconium ileus in the newborn.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'What would the nurse teaching an asthmatic child the technique of pursed-lip breathing include? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Inhale deeply through nose with mouth closed.',c:true,r:'Correct. The technique requires that breath be inhaled through the nose and exhaled through pursed lips in a nonforceful manner. The exhalation should be twice as long as the inhalation.'},
{t:'Make exhalation twice as long as inhalation.',c:true,r:'Correct. The technique requires that breath be inhaled through the nose and exhaled through pursed lips in a nonforceful manner. The exhalation should be twice as long as the inhalation.'},
{t:'Use medicated inhaler prior to perform breathing exercise.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The technique requires that breath be inhaled through the nose and exhaled through pursed lips in a nonforceful manner. The exhalation should be twice as long as the inhalation.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Inhale deeply through nose with mouth closed. / Make exhalation twice as long as inhalation. / Exhale through mouth as if whistling.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Exhale through mouth as if whistling.',c:true,r:'Correct. The technique requires that breath be inhaled through the nose and exhaled through pursed lips in a nonforceful manner. The exhalation should be twice as long as the inhalation.'},
{t:'Exhale forcefully.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The technique requires that breath be inhaled through the nose and exhaled through pursed lips in a nonforceful manner. The exhalation should be twice as long as the inhalation.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Inhale deeply through nose with mouth closed. / Make exhalation twice as long as inhalation. / Exhale through mouth as if whistling.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'A toddler must maintain bed rest for the diagnosis of pneumonia. What actions will the nurse implement? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Maintain strict bed rest.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Confinement to bed for a child does not always result in physical rest. In pediatrics, "bed rest" means providing play therapy that promotes minimal activity. The nurse should consider the age and developmental level of the child and the activity level involved in the play when designing appropriate activities and guiding parents in the home care of their child.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Consider age. / Assess developmental level. / Implement light play activities.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Consider age.',c:true,r:'Correct. Confinement to bed for a child does not always result in physical rest. In pediatrics, "bed rest" means providing play therapy that promotes minimal activity. The nurse should consider the age and developmental level of the child and the activity level involved in the play when designing appropriate activities and guiding parents in the home care of their child.'},
{t:'Assess developmental level.',c:true,r:'Correct. Confinement to bed for a child does not always result in physical rest. In pediatrics, "bed rest" means providing play therapy that promotes minimal activity. The nurse should consider the age and developmental level of the child and the activity level involved in the play when designing appropriate activities and guiding parents in the home care of their child.'},
{t:'Implement light play activities.',c:true,r:'Correct. Confinement to bed for a child does not always result in physical rest. In pediatrics, "bed rest" means providing play therapy that promotes minimal activity. The nurse should consider the age and developmental level of the child and the activity level involved in the play when designing appropriate activities and guiding parents in the home care of their child.'},
{t:'Provide hypnotic medication as ordered.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Confinement to bed for a child does not always result in physical rest. In pediatrics, "bed rest" means providing play therapy that promotes minimal activity. The nurse should consider the age and developmental level of the child and the activity level involved in the play when designing appropriate activities and guiding parents in the home care of their child.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Consider age. / Assess developmental level. / Implement light play activities.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'The school nurse suspects a first-grade student has sinusitis. Which symptoms might lead the nurse to this suspicion? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Child reports tooth pain.',c:true,r:'Correct. The proximity of the sinus to the tooth roots often results in tooth pain when the sinus is infected. The maxillary and ethmoid sinuses are most often involved in childhood sinusitis. Therefore, the signs and symptoms of sinusitis in children are different from those in adults, depending on the age of the child and which sinus is fully developed. An acute sinusitis is suspected when an upper respiratory infection lasts longer than 10 days, with a daytime cough. Halitosis is often present. Untreated sinusitis can lead to periorbital cellulitis. Severe wheezing is not indicative of sinusitis.'},
{t:'Severe wheezing is auscultated on inspiration.',c:false,r:'Severe wheezing is not indicative of sinusitis.'},
{t:'Child reports, "I have had a cold for 2 weeks."',c:true,r:'Correct. The proximity of the sinus to the tooth roots often results in tooth pain when the sinus is infected. The maxillary and ethmoid sinuses are most often involved in childhood sinusitis. Therefore, the signs and symptoms of sinusitis in children are different from those in adults, depending on the age of the child and which sinus is fully developed. An acute sinusitis is suspected when an upper respiratory infection lasts longer than 10 days, with a daytime cough. Halitosis is often present. Untreated sinusitis can lead to periorbital cellulitis. Severe wheezing is not indicative of sinusitis.'},
{t:'Nurse observes periorbital swelling.',c:true,r:'Correct. The proximity of the sinus to the tooth roots often results in tooth pain when the sinus is infected. The maxillary and ethmoid sinuses are most often involved in childhood sinusitis. Therefore, the signs and symptoms of sinusitis in children are different from those in adults, depending on the age of the child and which sinus is fully developed. An acute sinusitis is suspected when an upper respiratory infection lasts longer than 10 days, with a daytime cough. Halitosis is often present. Untreated sinusitis can lead to periorbital cellulitis. Severe wheezing is not indicative of sinusitis.'},
{t:'Halitosis is present.',c:true,r:'Correct. The proximity of the sinus to the tooth roots often results in tooth pain when the sinus is infected. The maxillary and ethmoid sinuses are most often involved in childhood sinusitis. Therefore, the signs and symptoms of sinusitis in children are different from those in adults, depending on the age of the child and which sinus is fully developed. An acute sinusitis is suspected when an upper respiratory infection lasts longer than 10 days, with a daytime cough. Halitosis is often present. Untreated sinusitis can lead to periorbital cellulitis. Severe wheezing is not indicative of sinusitis.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse is caring for a 4-year-old child diagnosed with H. influenzae type B. Which signs and symptoms exhibited by the child would alert the nurse to suspect epiglottitis? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Harsh cough',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Restlessness / Edematous epiglottis / Drooling</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Restlessness',c:true,r:'Correct. H. influenzae type B and most often occurs in children 3 to 6 years of age. It can occur in any season. The course is rapid and progressive. The onset of epiglottitis is abrupt, and the child presents with classic symptoms. The child insists on sitting up, leans forward with the mouth open, and drools saliva because of the difficulty in swallowing. The child appears wide-eyed, anxious, and restless, and he or she may emit a froglike croaking sound on inspiration. Cough is absent. Inspection of the throat shows an enlarged, reddened edematous epiglottis much like a "beefy-red thumb." However, the examining tongue blade may trigger a laryngospasm and result in sudden respiratory arrest.'},
{t:'Edematous epiglottis',c:true,r:'Correct. H. influenzae type B and most often occurs in children 3 to 6 years of age. It can occur in any season. The course is rapid and progressive. The onset of epiglottitis is abrupt, and the child presents with classic symptoms. The child insists on sitting up, leans forward with the mouth open, and drools saliva because of the difficulty in swallowing. The child appears wide-eyed, anxious, and restless, and he or she may emit a froglike croaking sound on inspiration. Cough is absent. Inspection of the throat shows an enlarged, reddened edematous epiglottis much like a "beefy-red thumb." However, the examining tongue blade may trigger a laryngospasm and result in sudden respiratory arrest.'},
{t:'Child insists on lying down',c:false,r:'The child insists on sitting up, leans forward with the mouth open, and drools saliva because of the difficulty in swallowing.'},
{t:'Drooling',c:true,r:'Correct. H. influenzae type B and most often occurs in children 3 to 6 years of age. It can occur in any season. The course is rapid and progressive. The onset of epiglottitis is abrupt, and the child presents with classic symptoms. The child insists on sitting up, leans forward with the mouth open, and drools saliva because of the difficulty in swallowing. The child appears wide-eyed, anxious, and restless, and he or she may emit a froglike croaking sound on inspiration. Cough is absent. Inspection of the throat shows an enlarged, reddened edematous epiglottis much like a "beefy-red thumb." However, the examining tongue blade may trigger a laryngospasm and result in sudden respiratory arrest.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 25',topic:'Respiratory (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'What will the nurse discourage when providing education to parents of a child with asthma? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Stuffed toys',c:true,r:'Correct. Use of stuffed toys is discouraged due to potential allergens. Basketball might not be well tolerated because of the constant physical exertion. Certain pets are encouraged, gymnastics is usually well tolerated, and cotton blankets are recommended for children with asthma.'},
{t:'Pet ownership',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Use of stuffed toys is discouraged due to potential allergens. Basketball might not be well tolerated because of the constant physical exertion. Certain pets are encouraged, gymnastics is usually well tolerated, and cotton blankets are recommended for children with asthma.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Stuffed toys / Basketball</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Gymnastics',c:false,r:'Certain pets are encouraged, gymnastics is usually well tolerated, and cotton blankets are recommended for children with asthma.'},
{t:'Basketball',c:true,r:'Correct. Use of stuffed toys is discouraged due to potential allergens. Basketball might not be well tolerated because of the constant physical exertion. Certain pets are encouraged, gymnastics is usually well tolerated, and cotton blankets are recommended for children with asthma.'},
{t:'Cotton blankets',c:false,r:'Certain pets are encouraged, gymnastics is usually well tolerated, and cotton blankets are recommended for children with asthma.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Respiratory Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 25 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'What does the nurse explain that a ventricular septal defect will allow?',
opts:[
{t:'Blood to shunt left to right, causing increased pulmonary flow and no cyanosis',c:true,r:'Correct. Pulmonary blood flow is increased when a ventricular septal defect exists. The blood shifts from left to right because of the higher pressure in the left ventricle. This particular shift does not cause cyanosis.'},
{t:'Blood to shunt right to left, causing decreased pulmonary flow and cyanosis',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Pulmonary blood flow is increased when a ventricular septal defect exists. The blood shifts from left to right because of the higher pressure in the left ventricle. This particular shift does not cause cyanosis.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Blood to shunt left to right, causing increased pulmonary flow and no cyanosis</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'No shunting because of high pressure in the left ventricle',c:false,r:'The blood shifts from left to right because of the higher pressure in the left ventricle.'},
{t:'Increased pressure in the left atrium, impeding circulation of oxygenated blood in the circulating volume',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Pulmonary blood flow is increased when a ventricular septal defect exists. The blood shifts from left to right because of the higher pressure in the left ventricle. This particular shift does not cause cyanosis.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Blood to shunt left to right, causing increased pulmonary flow and no cyanosis</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'Which assessment would lead the nurse to suspect that a newborn infant has a ventricular septal defect?',
opts:[
{t:'A loud, harsh murmur with a systolic thrill',c:true,r:'Correct. A loud, harsh murmur combined with a systolic thrill is characteristic of a ventricular septal defect.'},
{t:'Cyanosis when crying',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>A loud, harsh murmur combined with a systolic thrill is characteristic of a ventricular septal defect.</i> The keyed answer is <b>A loud, harsh murmur with a systolic thrill</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Blood pressure higher in the arms than in the legs',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>A loud, harsh murmur combined with a systolic thrill is characteristic of a ventricular septal defect.</i> The keyed answer is <b>A loud, harsh murmur with a systolic thrill</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'A machinery-like murmur',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>A loud, harsh murmur combined with a systolic thrill is characteristic of a ventricular septal defect.</i> The keyed answer is <b>A loud, harsh murmur with a systolic thrill</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'What finding would the nurse expect when measuring blood pressure on all four extremities of a child with coarctation of the aorta?',
opts:[
{t:'Blood pressure higher on the right side',c:false,r:'The characteristic symptoms of coarctation of the aorta are a marked difference in blood pressure and pulses between the upper and lower extremities.'},
{t:'Blood pressure higher on the left side',c:false,r:'The characteristic symptoms of coarctation of the aorta are a marked difference in blood pressure and pulses between the upper and lower extremities.'},
{t:'Blood pressure lower in the arms than in the legs',c:false,r:'The characteristic symptoms of coarctation of the aorta are a marked difference in blood pressure and pulses between the upper and lower extremities.'},
{t:'Blood pressure lower in the legs than in the arms',c:true,r:'Correct. The characteristic symptoms of coarctation of the aorta are a marked difference in blood pressure and pulses between the upper and lower extremities. Pressure is increased proximal to the defect and decreased distal to the coarctation.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'A father asks why his child with tetralogy of Fallot seems to favor a squatting position. What is the nurse\'s best response?',
opts:[
{t:'Squatting increases the return of venous blood back to the heart.',c:true,r:'Correct. The squatting position allows the child to breathe more easily because systemic venous return is increased.'},
{t:'Squatting decreases arterial blood flow away from the heart.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The squatting position allows the child to breathe more easily because systemic venous return is increased.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Squatting increases the return of venous blood back to the heart.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Squatting is a common resting position when a child is tachycardic.',c:false,r:'The squatting position allows the child to breathe more easily because systemic venous return is increased.'},
{t:'Squatting increases the workload of the heart.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The squatting position allows the child to breathe more easily because systemic venous return is increased.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Squatting increases the return of venous blood back to the heart.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'An infant is experiencing dyspnea related to patent ductus arteriosus (PDA). What does the nurse understand regarding why dyspnea occurs?',
opts:[
{t:'Blood is circulated through the lungs again, causing pulmonary circulatory congestion.',c:true,r:'Correct. When PDA is present, oxygenated blood recycles through the lungs, overburdening the pulmonary circulation.'},
{t:'Blood is shunted past the pulmonary circulation, causing pulmonary hypoxia.',c:false,r:'When PDA is present, oxygenated blood recycles through the lungs, overburdening the pulmonary circulation.'},
{t:'Blood is shunted past cardiac arteries, causing myocardial hypoxia.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>When PDA is present, oxygenated blood recycles through the lungs, overburdening the pulmonary circulation.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Blood is circulated through the lungs again, causing pulmonary circulatory congestion.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Blood is circulated through the ductus from the pulmonary artery to the aorta, bypassing the left side of the heart.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>When PDA is present, oxygenated blood recycles through the lungs, overburdening the pulmonary circulation.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Blood is circulated through the lungs again, causing pulmonary circulatory congestion.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'Which is the most appropriate nursing action related to the administration of digoxin (Lanoxin) to an infant?',
opts:[
{t:'Counting the apical rate for 30 seconds before administering the medication',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>As a rule, if the pulse rate of an infant is less than 100 beats/minute, the medication is withheld and the physician is notified.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Withholding a dose if the apical heart rate is less than 100 beats/minute</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Withholding a dose if the apical heart rate is less than 100 beats/minute',c:true,r:'Correct. As a rule, if the pulse rate of an infant is less than 100 beats/minute, the medication is withheld and the physician is notified.'},
{t:'Repeating a dose if the child vomits within 30 minutes of the previous dose',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>As a rule, if the pulse rate of an infant is less than 100 beats/minute, the medication is withheld and the physician is notified.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Withholding a dose if the apical heart rate is less than 100 beats/minute</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Checking respiratory rate and blood pressure before each dose',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>As a rule, if the pulse rate of an infant is less than 100 beats/minute, the medication is withheld and the physician is notified.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Withholding a dose if the apical heart rate is less than 100 beats/minute</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
