{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse is caring for a child with a diagnosis of Kawasaki disease. The child\'s parent asks the nurse, "How does Kawasaki disease affect my child\'s heart and blood vessels?" On what understanding is the nurse\'s response based?',
opts:[
{t:'Inflammation weakens blood vessels, leading to aneurysm.',c:true,r:'Correct. Inflammation of vessels weakens the walls of the vessels and often results in aneurysm.'},
{t:'Increased lipid levels lead to the development of atherosclerosis.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Inflammation of vessels weakens the walls of the vessels and often results in aneurysm.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Inflammation weakens blood vessels, leading to aneurysm.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Untreated disease causes mitral valve stenosis.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Inflammation of vessels weakens the walls of the vessels and often results in aneurysm.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Inflammation weakens blood vessels, leading to aneurysm.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Altered blood flow increases cardiac workload with resulting heart failure.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Inflammation of vessels weakens the walls of the vessels and often results in aneurysm.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Inflammation weakens blood vessels, leading to aneurysm.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The nurse explained how to position an infant with tetralogy of Fallot if the infant suddenly becomes cyanotic. Which statement by the father leads the nurse to determine he understood the instructions?',
opts:[
{t:'"If the baby turns blue, I will hold him against my shoulder with his knees bent up toward his chest."',c:true,r:'Correct. In the event of a paroxysmal hypercyanotic or "tet" spell, the infant should be placed in a knee-chest position.'},
{t:'"If the baby turns blue, I will lay him down on a firm surface with his head lower than the rest of his body."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>In the event of a paroxysmal hypercyanotic or "tet" spell, the infant should be placed in a knee-chest position.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"If the baby turns blue, I will hold him against my shoulder with his knees bent up toward his chest."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'"If the baby turns blue, I will immediately put the baby upright in an infant seat."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>In the event of a paroxysmal hypercyanotic or "tet" spell, the infant should be placed in a knee-chest position.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"If the baby turns blue, I will hold him against my shoulder with his knees bent up toward his chest."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'"If the baby turns blue, I will put the baby in supine position with his head elevated."',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>In the event of a paroxysmal hypercyanotic or "tet" spell, the infant should be placed in a knee-chest position.</i> The keyed answer is <b>"If the baby turns blue, I will hold him against my shoulder with his knees bent up toward his chest."</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Evaluation'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'The parent of a 1-year-old child with tetralogy of Fallot asks the nurse, "Why do my child\'s fingertips look like that?" On what understanding does the nurse base a response?',
opts:[
{t:'Clubbing occurs as a result of untreated congestive heart failure.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Clubbing of the fingers develops in response to chronic hypoxia.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Clubbing occurs as a result of chronic hypoxia.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Clubbing occurs as a result of a left-to-right shunting of blood.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Clubbing of the fingers develops in response to chronic hypoxia.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Clubbing occurs as a result of chronic hypoxia.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Clubbing occurs as a result of decreased cardiac output.',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Clubbing of the fingers develops in response to chronic hypoxia.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Clubbing occurs as a result of chronic hypoxia.</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Clubbing occurs as a result of chronic hypoxia.',c:true,r:'Correct. Clubbing of the fingers develops in response to chronic hypoxia.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'A child has an elevated antistreptolysin O (ASO) titer. Which combination of symptoms, in conjunction with this finding, would confirm a diagnosis of rheumatic fever?',
opts:[
{t:'Subcutaneous nodules and fever',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The presence of two major Jones criteria would indicate a high probability of rheumatic fever.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Painful, tender joints, and carditis</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Painful, tender joints, and carditis',c:true,r:'Correct. The presence of two major Jones criteria would indicate a high probability of rheumatic fever.'},
{t:'Erythema marginatum and arthralgia',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The presence of two major Jones criteria would indicate a high probability of rheumatic fever.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Painful, tender joints, and carditis</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Chorea and elevated sedimentation rate',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The presence of two major Jones criteria would indicate a high probability of rheumatic fever.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Painful, tender joints, and carditis</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'An infant with congestive heart failure is receiving digoxin (Lanoxin). What does the nurse recognize as a sign of digoxin toxicity?',
opts:[
{t:'Restlessness',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Symptoms of digoxin toxicity include nausea, vomiting, anorexia, irregularity in pulse rate and rhythm, and a sudden change in pulse.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Vomiting</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Decreased respiratory rate',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Symptoms of digoxin toxicity include nausea, vomiting, anorexia, irregularity in pulse rate and rhythm, and a sudden change in pulse.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Vomiting</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Increased urinary output',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Symptoms of digoxin toxicity include nausea, vomiting, anorexia, irregularity in pulse rate and rhythm, and a sudden change in pulse.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Vomiting</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Vomiting',c:true,r:'Correct. Symptoms of digoxin toxicity include nausea, vomiting, anorexia, irregularity in pulse rate and rhythm, and a sudden change in pulse.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'Through what does the infant born with hypoplastic left heart syndrome acquire oxygenated blood?',
opts:[
{t:'The patent ductus arteriosus',c:false,r:'Because the right side of the heart must take over pumping blood to both the lungs and systemic circulation, the ductus arteriosus must remain open to shunt the oxygenated blood from the lungs.'},
{t:'A ventricular septal defect',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Because the right side of the heart must take over pumping blood to both the lungs and systemic circulation, the ductus arteriosus must remain open to shunt the oxygenated blood from the lungs.</i> The keyed answer is <b>An atrial septal defect</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'The closure of the foramen ovale',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Because the right side of the heart must take over pumping blood to both the lungs and systemic circulation, the ductus arteriosus must remain open to shunt the oxygenated blood from the lungs.</i> The keyed answer is <b>An atrial septal defect</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'An atrial septal defect',c:true,r:'Correct. Because the right side of the heart must take over pumping blood to both the lungs and systemic circulation, the ductus arteriosus must remain open to shunt the oxygenated blood from the lungs.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning<br><br><b style="color:#fca5a5">&#9888; THE BANK&rsquo;S KEY IS WRONG ON THIS ONE.</b> This item contradicts itself. The bank keys <b>atrial septal defect</b>, but its own rationale says the <b>ductus arteriosus</b> must stay open to shunt the blood &mdash; which is option a. Blood does cross the atrial septum in this defect, so both routes matter, but the rationale does not support the key it prints.'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
