{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'MC',vuln:'low',
stem:'A pediatric patient is scheduled for a noninvasive procedure to determine if his heart is structurally normal and to localize a murmur. What diagnostic test does the nurse anticipate?',
opts:[
{t:'Barium swallow',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Echocardiography is a noninvasive procedure that localizes murmurs and determines if the heart is structurally normal.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Echocardiogram</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Chest x-ray',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Echocardiography is a noninvasive procedure that localizes murmurs and determines if the heart is structurally normal.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Echocardiogram</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Electrocardiogram',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Echocardiography is a noninvasive procedure that localizes murmurs and determines if the heart is structurally normal.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Echocardiogram</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Echocardiogram',c:true,r:'Correct. Echocardiography is a noninvasive procedure that localizes murmurs and determines if the heart is structurally normal.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'How would the nurse caring for an infant with congestive heart failure (CHF) modify feeding techniques to adapt for the child\'s weakness and fatigue? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Feeding more frequently with smaller feedings',c:true,r:'Correct. Infants with CHF fatigue easily. Feeding can be given more frequently in smaller amounts through a soft, large-holed nipple. Formulas with a denser caloric content can be offered. The child may be encouraged to nurse if he or she is held.'},
{t:'Using a soft nipple with enlarged holes',c:true,r:'Correct. Infants with CHF fatigue easily. Feeding can be given more frequently in smaller amounts through a soft, large-holed nipple. Formulas with a denser caloric content can be offered. The child may be encouraged to nurse if he or she is held.'},
{t:'Holding and cuddling the child during feeding',c:true,r:'Correct. Infants with CHF fatigue easily. Feeding can be given more frequently in smaller amounts through a soft, large-holed nipple. Formulas with a denser caloric content can be offered. The child may be encouraged to nurse if he or she is held.'},
{t:'Substituting glucose water for formula',c:false,r:'The bank does not argue this one down directly. The keyed answer is <b>Feeding more frequently with smaller feedings / Using a soft nipple with enlarged holes / Holding and cuddling the child during feeding / Offering high-caloric formula</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option. If you cannot say it, that is the gap.'},
{t:'Offering high-caloric formula',c:true,r:'Correct. Infants with CHF fatigue easily. Feeding can be given more frequently in smaller amounts through a soft, large-holed nipple. Formulas with a denser caloric content can be offered. The child may be encouraged to nurse if he or she is held.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'What are the four structural heart anomalies that make up the tetralogy of Fallot? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Hypertrophied right ventricle',c:true,r:'Correct. The four anomalies that comprise tetralogy of Fallot are hypertrophied right ventricle, patent ductus arteriosus, stenosis of pulmonary artery, and dextroposition of the aorta.'},
{t:'Patent ductus arteriosus',c:true,r:'Correct. The four anomalies that comprise tetralogy of Fallot are hypertrophied right ventricle, patent ductus arteriosus, stenosis of pulmonary artery, and dextroposition of the aorta.'},
{t:'Ventral septal defect',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The four anomalies that comprise tetralogy of Fallot are hypertrophied right ventricle, patent ductus arteriosus, stenosis of pulmonary artery, and dextroposition of the aorta.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Hypertrophied right ventricle / Patent ductus arteriosus / Narrowing of pulmonary artery / Dextroposition of aorta</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Narrowing of pulmonary artery',c:true,r:'Correct. The four anomalies that comprise tetralogy of Fallot are hypertrophied right ventricle, patent ductus arteriosus, stenosis of pulmonary artery, and dextroposition of the aorta.'},
{t:'Dextroposition of aorta',c:true,r:'Correct. The four anomalies that comprise tetralogy of Fallot are hypertrophied right ventricle, patent ductus arteriosus, stenosis of pulmonary artery, and dextroposition of the aorta.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation<br><br><b style="color:#fca5a5">&#9888; THE BANK&rsquo;S KEY IS WRONG ON THIS ONE.</b> This item is keyed wrong. The bank marks <b>patent ductus arteriosus</b> as one of the four and marks the septal defect wrong. Tetralogy of Fallot is <b>VSD, pulmonary stenosis, overriding aorta, and right ventricular hypertrophy</b>. A PDA can coexist, but it is not one of the four. Learn the real four.'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'What assessment(s) in a child with tetralogy of Fallot would indicate the child is experiencing a paroxysmal hypercyanotic episode? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Spontaneous cyanosis',c:true,r:'Correct. Indicators of a paroxysmal hypercyanotic episode or a "tet" episode are spontaneous cyanosis, dyspnea, weakness, and syncope.'},
{t:'Dyspnea',c:true,r:'Correct. Indicators of a paroxysmal hypercyanotic episode or a "tet" episode are spontaneous cyanosis, dyspnea, weakness, and syncope.'},
{t:'Weakness',c:true,r:'Correct. Indicators of a paroxysmal hypercyanotic episode or a "tet" episode are spontaneous cyanosis, dyspnea, weakness, and syncope.'},
{t:'Dry cough',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Indicators of a paroxysmal hypercyanotic episode or a "tet" episode are spontaneous cyanosis, dyspnea, weakness, and syncope.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Spontaneous cyanosis / Dyspnea / Weakness / Syncope</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Syncope',c:true,r:'Correct. Indicators of a paroxysmal hypercyanotic episode or a "tet" episode are spontaneous cyanosis, dyspnea, weakness, and syncope.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Data Collection'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'Which congenital cardiac defect(s) cause(s) increased pulmonary blood flow? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Atrial septal defects (ASDs)',c:true,r:'Correct. The congenital heart defects that cause increased pulmonary blood flow are ASDs, VSDs, and patent ductus arteriosus.'},
{t:'Tetralogy of Fallot',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The congenital heart defects that cause increased pulmonary blood flow are ASDs, VSDs, and patent ductus arteriosus.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Atrial septal defects (ASDs) / Patent ductus arteriosus / Ventricular septal defects (VSDs)</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Dextroposition of aorta',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>The congenital heart defects that cause increased pulmonary blood flow are ASDs, VSDs, and patent ductus arteriosus.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Atrial septal defects (ASDs) / Patent ductus arteriosus / Ventricular septal defects (VSDs)</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Patent ductus arteriosus',c:true,r:'Correct. The congenital heart defects that cause increased pulmonary blood flow are ASDs, VSDs, and patent ductus arteriosus.'},
{t:'Ventricular septal defects (VSDs)',c:true,r:'Correct. The congenital heart defects that cause increased pulmonary blood flow are ASDs, VSDs, and patent ductus arteriosus.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Implementation'},

{ch:'Ch 26',topic:'Cardiovascular (peds)',bank:true,type:'SATA',vuln:'low',
stem:'A 16-year-old patient is diagnosed with primary hypertension. What risk factors does the nurse mention when providing education on this diagnosis to the patient and his family? (Select all that apply.)',
opts:[
{t:'Heredity',c:true,r:'Correct. Primary, or essential, hypertension implies that no known underlying disease is present. Nevertheless, heredity, obesity, stress, and a poor diet and exercise pattern can contribute to any type of hypertension.'},
{t:'Stress',c:true,r:'Correct. Primary, or essential, hypertension implies that no known underlying disease is present. Nevertheless, heredity, obesity, stress, and a poor diet and exercise pattern can contribute to any type of hypertension.'},
{t:'Congenital defect',c:false,r:'Not the keyed answer. What the bank says about this item: <i>Primary, or essential, hypertension implies that no known underlying disease is present. Nevertheless, heredity, obesity, stress, and a poor diet and exercise pattern can contribute to any type of hypertension.</i> The keyed answer is <b>Heredity / Stress / Obesity / Poor diet</b> &mdash; say out loud why that beats this option.'},
{t:'Obesity',c:true,r:'Correct. Primary, or essential, hypertension implies that no known underlying disease is present. Nevertheless, heredity, obesity, stress, and a poor diet and exercise pattern can contribute to any type of hypertension.'},
{t:'Poor diet',c:true,r:'Correct. Primary, or essential, hypertension implies that no known underlying disease is present. Nevertheless, heredity, obesity, stress, and a poor diet and exercise pattern can contribute to any type of hypertension.'}],
teach:'<b>The Child with a Cardiovascular Disorder</b> &middot; Leifer 10e test bank, Ch 26 item. Cognitive level: Cognitive. Bank tags: Nursing Process Step: Planning'},

